1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,200
Today, we will be discussing the parcel problem.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:18,500
A parcel map shows every piece of land in a county,
with a record of who owns it.

3
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:31,000
Five county offices use that map every day,
each for a different job.

4
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:45,900
Each office keeps its own copy of the parcel map
and refreshes it on its own schedule.

5
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:57,900
On Monday the assessor splits one lot in two.
Only the assessor's copy shows it.

6
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:14,900
The other copies catch up days or weeks later.
Until then, five maps give different answers.

7
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:26,400
Even the column that holds the parcel ID
has a different name in every copy.

8
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:40,300
Each copy is a hosted layer: the online version
of the map that web maps and apps read.

9
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:54,800
To refresh it, the office overwrites the whole layer.
Every row is deleted and loaded again.

10
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:09,300
But only 312 parcels changed that day.
The other 47,900 were rewritten for nothing.

11
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:21,800
And once field crews turn on offline sync,
the layer refuses to be overwritten at all.

12
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:33,200
So there are three separate problems here.

13
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:44,700
Each one has a fix that GIS teams already use.

14
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:57,600
Start with one database that holds the official parcels.
This is the source of record.

15
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:12,100
Staff edit it through one editing service,
from a desk or from a phone in the field.

16
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:25,600
The database stamps every change with who made it
and when. That stamp is called editor tracking.

17
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:40,000
Each night a job asks the database one question:
what changed since the last run?

18
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:54,500
It checks those changes before anyone sees them.
Row counts, shapes and column names must all pass.

19
00:03:55,100 --> 00:04:09,000
Then it sends only those 312 changes to the hosted layer.
Nothing is overwritten.

20
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:23,500
The same changes go into two files: one for analysts,
and one for web maps outside ArcGIS.

21
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:37,900
Now every office reads from those outputs.
Nobody keeps a copy of their own.

22
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:54,900
The assessor splits a lot on Monday afternoon.
By Tuesday morning, all five maps show it.

23
00:04:56,400 --> 00:05:10,300
Before: five copies and five refresh jobs.
After: one source of record and one nightly job.

24
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:22,300
All five maps now show the same parcels,
and each refresh moves 99 percent fewer rows.

25
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,500
That was the parcel problem. Thank you for watching.
